WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.583
A student's SQL answer passes

2
00:00:02.661 --> 00:00:05.180
the test cases, but is it right?

3
00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.076
Two queries are equivalent if they return

4
00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:10.250
the same result on every database.

5
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.070
Proving that is hard

6
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.520
but one counterexample refutes it.

7
00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:19.430
Polygon, from PLDI 2025, finds small ones.

8
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.639
It encodes both queries over

9
00:00:21.706 --> 00:00:23.034
a few symbolic rows

10
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.826
and asks an SMT solver for

11
00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:26.420
values that split them.

12
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.780
The task's public example is

13
00:00:28.844 --> 00:00:30.496
a coding-exercise problem

14
00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:31.704
for each customer

15
00:00:31.704 --> 00:00:34.120
count the visits with no transaction.

16
00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.710
The reference uses NOT IN.

17
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.454
A submitted answer uses a LEFT JOIN

18
00:00:39.454 --> 00:00:42.180
and keeps the rows whose amount is NULL.

19
00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.583
On a database with two visits

20
00:00:44.654 --> 00:00:46.070
and one transaction

21
00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.690
both give customer seven, one visit.

22
00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.620
So we search all small databases

23
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.760
smallest first

24
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.110
with at most two rows per table.

25
00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:56.712
With NULLs allowed

26
00:00:56.712 --> 00:00:59.347
the third database already splits them

27
00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:01.060
a visit with no customer.

28
00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:02.947
The answer counts zero

29
00:01:02.947 --> 00:01:05.486
because counting a column skips NULL

30
00:01:05.486 --> 00:01:07.490
and the reference counts one.

31
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.315
Forbid a NULL customer

32
00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:11.625
and the fifth database splits them

33
00:01:11.689 --> 00:01:13.549
a NULL amount keeps its visit

34
00:01:13.613 --> 00:01:14.960
in the answer's join.

35
00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.075
Forbid NULLs altogether

36
00:01:17.075 --> 00:01:19.870
and all thirty-seven databases agree.

37
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.050
Pause and predict.

38
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.306
Can test data without NULLs

39
00:01:24.376 --> 00:01:26.000
ever catch this answer?

40
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:30.190
No.

41
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.100
Both bugs hide in NULL values.

42
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.900
Polygon lets the solver choose the values.

43
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.398
But encoding every behaviour of a query at once

44
00:01:40.465 --> 00:01:43.010
makes a formula too big to solve fast.

45
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.840
So Polygon under-approximates.

46
00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:47.838
It commits to some choices

47
00:01:47.838 --> 00:01:49.790
and solves the smaller formula.

48
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.780
If that has no solution

49
00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:53.489
the unsat core names the

50
00:01:53.558 --> 00:01:55.061
choices that conflict

51
00:01:55.061 --> 00:01:57.180
and Polygon widens only those.

52
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.303
The task removes Polygon's search

53
00:02:00.303 --> 00:02:01.200
end to end.

54
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.479
A refutation counts only if a real database

55
00:02:04.547 --> 00:02:06.590
engine sees different results.

56
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.721
There are sixteen wrong submitted answers

57
00:02:09.787 --> 00:02:11.773
three tasks that split queries

58
00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:14.620
into two equal groups, and two hard pairs.

59
00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.243
To pass, a run must refute wherever

60
00:02:17.307 --> 00:02:18.541
the reference does

61
00:02:18.541 --> 00:02:21.138
and take at most twenty percent longer

62
00:02:21.138 --> 00:02:23.930
or thirty milliseconds, whichever is more.

63
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.533
On the hard pairs

64
00:02:25.533 --> 00:02:28.040
it must be more than twice as fast.

65
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.940
Four of sixteen models passed.

66
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.450
GPT-6.1 Terra and Kimi K3.1

67
00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.730
Pro shared the best score, ninety-eight

68
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.810
against ninety-five point

69
00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:40.650
six for the reference.

70
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.071
Claude Opus 5.5 refuted all

71
00:02:43.148 --> 00:02:44.758
twenty-one workloads

72
00:02:44.758 --> 00:02:47.520
but was too slow on eleven of them

73
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.440
and failed.

74
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:50.796
GPT-6.1 Sol was faster than

75
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.207
every passing run

76
00:02:52.207 --> 00:02:54.436
but it missed one refutation

77
00:02:54.436 --> 00:02:57.110
and its score fell to ninety-three.

78
00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.130
So: one database refutes an equivalence.

79
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.600
NULL values hide bugs from test cases.

80
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.542
And commit to choices

81
00:03:06.542 --> 00:03:09.510
then widen only where a conflict points.

82
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.660
As an exercise, add a NOT NULL rule to the

83
00:03:12.727 --> 00:03:14.127
customer column only

84
00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:16.660
and find the smallest counterexample.

85
00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.770
Next, Glenside.
